  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Stalled front bisects the Hwy 64 corridor this \nafternoon with a secondary leftover outflow boundary noted along\nthe Hwy 70 corridor. These two boundaries have been the focus \nfor ongoing shower and thunderstorm activity this afternoon. \nGiven the ongoing cloud cover temps outside of the Onslow and \nDuplin Counties have not increased much today which has kept the\narea in the mid 80s to low 90s today. Have kept heat headlines \nin place across Duplin and Onslow Counties. As we get into \ntomorrow and Wed, less cloud cover and warmer temps as highs get\ninto the upper 80s to mid 90s. Combined with dewpoints in the \nmid to upper 70s each day, heat indices could range from\n105-109F each afternoon across a larger footprint of ENC. As a \nresult could see a broader heat headline tomorrow for ENC once \nthe ongoing heat advisory ends. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...An unsettled pattern will persist over the next few \ndays with several factors forecast to increase shower and \nthunderstorm chances this week. \n\nA stalled boundary currently bisecting Hwy 64 and a remnant\noutflow boundary along Hwy 70, combined with persistent inland \ntroughing, and plenty of moisture influx from the S and SW will \nkeep a cloudy and active forecast in place this afternoon and\nearly evening. Fortunately, the mid and upper level trough over\nNECONUS is pushing offshore this afternoon, ending the little \nmid and upper level dynamic support for organized convection. A \nfair amount of cloud coverage has dampened heating today from \nwhat we have experienced over the weekend with 2000-3000 J/kg \nMLCAPE currently noted across the FA this afternoon. Iso strong \nstorms with damaging wind gusts will be possible. PWATs still \ngreater than 2\" will keep threat for heavy rain which could lead\nto localized flooding in place through tonight. Tonight, the\noutflow boundary dissipates and the frontal boundary lifts back\nN, \"warm- sectoring\" the FA once again for Tue and Wed. \n\nTue features slight dip in available moisture with drier 850-700mb \nlayer getting pushed up the SECONUS coast ahead of the next upper \ntrough which will be pushing SE across the Ohio River Valley, \nleading to more typical summer-time PoPs of 20-40% through Tue \nnight. \n\nA strong front is forecast to approach and cross the area late Wed \ninto Thu, posing the next threat for severe weather. Upper level \npattern for this frontal passage appears more supportive of stronger \nstorms with a sharper trough aloft digging further S than this \nweekend's trough. Moisture increases ahead of this front with low to \nmidlevel flow opening up moisture advection channel from the E side \nof what is now Tropical Depression 2. The National Hurricane Center \ncontinues issuing advisories for TD2, but poses no tropical threat \nto ENC at this time, as it is forecast to meander slowly W across \nthe Nern Gulf of America.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Wed/Thu stronger front is forecast to make it \nthrough the entirety of the land area of ENC, but still stall \noffshore. This will lead to mostly N-NEerly flow late week into the \nweekend, cutting down on the heat (low to mid 80s/upper 60s splits \ninto the weekend). Severe threat appears minimal behind the midweek \nfront, but the multi day period of showers in the forecast could \nlead to hydro issues.",
